1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:37,306
High above our planet, a laboratory the size of a football field circles Earth every ninety minutes. That is the International Space Station, flying two hundred and fifty miles above the surface at seventeen thousand five hundred miles per hour. Sixteen times each day, the station laps the planet, and from its cupola, Earth looks like a living painting. The crew watches continents drift by, oceans swell, and clouds roll across the globe — a constant, turning view of the world we all share. From this height there are no borders drawn on the surface, only land and ocean and ice.

2
00:00:37,806 --> 00:01:10,794
The cupola is the station's window to the world. It is a dome built for observation, with seven windows that look out onto Earth, onto spacewalks, and onto arriving and departing spacecraft. Through those windows, astronauts watch hurricanes spin, volcanoes smoke, city lights bloom at night, and glaciers creep across the land. This is Earth observation from orbit, and it helps scientists understand our changing planet. The cupola has supported robotics operations, docking, and photography for more than a decade.

3
00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:37,373
Every ninety minutes, the station completes one orbit. That means the view outside the cupola flips from blazing daylight to the thin blue line of the atmosphere against the black of space, sixteen times every day. Astronauts watch the sun rise and set in rapid succession, a rhythm no one on the ground experiences. The protective shell of our atmosphere looks startlingly thin from up there — a fragile blue line between life and the void.

4
00:01:37,873 --> 00:01:58,621
The station is more than a factory in the sky. It is a classroom, a laboratory, and a proving ground, orbiting our planet while hosting nearly three hundred astronauts from across the globe. The research done here — in human health, in materials, in Earth science — prepares humanity for longer stays in space and for missions farther from home.

5
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:16,577
Astronauts who spend months aboard the station describe something called the overview effect. Seeing Earth without borders, a single fragile sphere hanging in the void, changes how they see home. National boundaries disappear from this height. What remains is one planet, shared by everyone.

6
00:02:17,077 --> 00:02:36,265
The cupola's seven windows give the crew a panoramic view few people will ever have. From there, they guide robotics operations, watch spacecraft dock, and photograph our world. Each image, each observation, adds to a record of Earth that stretches back decades and helps us track how the planet is changing.

7
00:02:36,765 --> 00:02:56,636
When you look at Earth from the cupola, there are no lines drawn on the surface. There is only land and ocean, cloud and ice, one interconnected system. The station's continuous presence in orbit — people living and working in space, day after day — reminds us that we are capable of looking after our home when we choose to.

8
00:02:57,136 --> 00:03:14,425
Two hundred and fifty miles up. Seventeen thousand five hundred miles per hour. Seven windows on the world. The International Space Station proves that, seen from orbit, Earth is not a collection of separate places — it is one home, shared by everyone who has ever lived.

